<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy of São Paulo</h1><p class="st-meta">1709 CE – 1821 CE · capital São Paulo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The royal captaincy created around São Paulo after the gold rush, from which the captaincies of Brazil's interior were carved and where independence was declared.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-emboabas">the War of the Emboabas</a> between Paulista pioneers and newcomers in the gold fields, the crown bought out the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-sao-vicente">Captaincy of São Vicente</a> and created the Captaincy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Minas</a> de Ouro in 1709, with São Paulo as its capital. It covered much of southern and central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The vast captaincy was soon divided: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> was separated in 1720, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Goiás</a> in 1744 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-mato-grosso">Mato Grosso</a> in 1748. In 1748 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> itself lost its governor and was placed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>. It was restored in 1765 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-antonio-de-sousa-botelho-mourao">Morgado de Mateus</a>, who promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cultivation. Paulista society was rooted in the bandeirante tradition, with mule trains linking the south to the mines.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1821 the captaincy became a province. On 7 September 1822, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipiranga-river">Ipiranga</a> stream in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>, Prince Pedro proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> independence, and the province joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a>, later becoming its economic powerhouse through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-of-sao-paulo.jpg" alt="Captaincy of São Paulo" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Tonyjeff, based on ancient national symbol. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_of_Portugal,_Brazil,_and_the_Algarves.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1709 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>São Paulo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Morgado de Mateus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Captaincy of São Paulo and Minas de Ouro</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1765&amp;focus=captaincy-of-sao-paulo" aria-label="Captaincy of São Paulo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3398 3009 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Captaincy of São Paulo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3718 3265L3765 3317M3718 3265L3944 2917M3718 3265L3814 3166M3718 3265L3714 2993M3718 3265L3454 2912"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3765 3317h0M3944 2917h0M3814 3166h0M3714 2993h0M3454 2912h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3718 3265h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3718 3265h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1765&amp;focus=captaincy-of-sao-paulo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
